<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Popular Front (Chile)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-france">Popular Front</a> was a coalition of Chilean centre and left parties, led by the Radical Party with the Socialists and Communists, formed in 1936. Its candidate Pedro Aguirre Cerda narrowly won the presidential election of 1938, and his government founded the development agency <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfo">CORFO</a> in 1939 to promote industry after the Chillán earthquake. The coalition broke up in 1941.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>From 1935 the Communist Party of Chile advocated a popular front, following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>'s line and hoping to win elections as such fronts had in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It toned down its revolutionary language, accepting compromise with bourgeois democracy and supporting industrial development. The Socialist Party remained sceptical and joined only when victory for the right-wing candidate Gustavo Ross seemed certain. Besides the Radical, Socialist and Communist parties, the coalition took in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> and the Radical Socialist Party, the trade-union confederation CTCH, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> movement united in the Frente Único Araucano and the feminist MEMCh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Presidential Convention of the Left met in the National Congress on 15-17 April 1938, with 400 Radical delegates, 330 Socialists, 120 Communists, 120 Democrats and 60 from the CTCH. No one reached the required 684 of 1,030 votes: Pedro Aguirre Cerda had 520, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marmaduke-grove">Marmaduke Grove</a> 360 and Elías Lafertte 150, until a Socialist congress withdrew Grove and backed Aguirre Cerda, who went on to defeat Ross.</p><p>In office the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-france">Popular Front</a> set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfo">CORFO</a> after the 1939 earthquake to run public works. It built 1,000 primary schools and created 3,000 teaching posts, and carried out reforms in social security, public health and workers' rights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a> served as minister of health. Aguirre Cerda named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-neruda">Pablo Neruda</a> special consul in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> for immigration, and Neruda organised the voyage of the SS Winnipeg, which brought 2,200 Spanish Republican refugees to Chile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After Aguirre Cerda's death in 1941 the coalition became the Democratic Alliance, which united the same parties for the presidential election of 1942. The Popular Front was the first of several centre-left governments that led Chile until 1952. Writers and artists backed it, among them Neruda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriela-mistral">Gabriela Mistral</a>, the Surrealist group La Mandrágora and the proletarian novelists of the Generation of 1938.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Popular Front (Chile)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1936 the <b>Popular Front</b> brought a 40-hour week and two weeks of paid holiday.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Popular Front</b> government elected in 1938 created the development agency CORFO in 1939, and the Christian Democrat Eduardo Frei Montalva expanded land reform in the 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Popular Front</b>'s victory in February 1936 Manuel Azaña formed a government and soon replaced Alcalá-Zamora as president.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
